WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:04.260
In this video, we're talking about one of the fundamental properties of algebra, which is called the

00:04.260 --> 00:05.340
associative property.

00:05.340 --> 00:09.960
And the associative property works for addition and multiplication, and it tells us that we can group

00:09.960 --> 00:13.650
things together in whichever order we want and we'll still get the correct answer.

00:13.650 --> 00:18.720
So for example, here, essentially we have a plus B plus C, the associative property tells us that

00:18.720 --> 00:23.790
if we group together A plus B and then we add seeds, the result of the group will get the same answer

00:23.790 --> 00:29.490
as grouping together B plus C and then adding A to the result of this B plus C group.

00:29.490 --> 00:33.750
So in other words, no matter how we've grouped it, we're still going to end up with A plus B plus

00:33.750 --> 00:37.350
C on the left and A plus B plus C on the right.

00:37.350 --> 00:38.820
They're going to be equal to each other.

00:38.820 --> 00:41.520
We can do this with real numbers and see that it's true.

00:41.520 --> 00:45.300
So for here we have three plus two plus one on both sides.

00:45.300 --> 00:49.230
We've grouped together the three and two and said, add the three and the two together.

00:49.230 --> 00:53.340
First over here on the right hand side, we said Add the two and the one together first.

00:53.340 --> 00:54.630
It doesn't matter how we do it.

00:54.630 --> 00:57.090
Over here on the left, we get three, plus two is five.

00:57.090 --> 01:01.740
So we have five plus one over here on the right hand side, we have three plus.

01:01.740 --> 01:03.750
And then inside the parentheses we have three.

01:03.750 --> 01:08.190
No matter how we do it, you can see that we're going to get six on the left and six on the right.

01:08.190 --> 01:13.410
So no matter how we grouped these terms together, we still got the same answer because the associative

01:13.410 --> 01:17.790
property tells us that the grouping doesn't matter when we're dealing with addition or multiplication.

01:17.790 --> 01:23.220
So looking at some examples for multiplication, we have A, times B, times C and whether we do eight

01:23.220 --> 01:28.980
times B first and then multiply that result by C or if we do B times C first and then multiply that

01:28.980 --> 01:30.540
result by A, it doesn't matter.

01:30.540 --> 01:37.080
We're still going to get a times B, times C equal to A, times B, times C, the associative property

01:37.080 --> 01:40.110
tells us that the grouping, the order doesn't matter.

01:40.320 --> 01:43.470
If we look at this with real numbers, we can see why it's true here.

01:43.470 --> 01:46.380
We've grouped together the three and the two on the left and on the right.

01:46.380 --> 01:48.150
We've grouped together the two and the one.

01:48.150 --> 01:50.640
So on the left hand side, three times two is six.

01:50.640 --> 01:52.080
So we'll get six times one.

01:52.080 --> 01:54.450
On the right hand side, two times one is two.

01:54.480 --> 01:56.400
So we'll get three times two.

01:56.400 --> 02:01.080
And of course, that just gives us six on the left and six on the right so they're equal to one another.

02:01.080 --> 02:05.520
So when it comes to addition and multiplication, the associative property tells you that the order

02:05.520 --> 02:09.000
of the addition and the order of the multiplication don't matter.
